= Development 

== This section will explain how the repository is build

This section will contain the instructions for creating a repository to allow users to use  "xbps-install -S yourpkg" to install packages from this repository.

=== What is a Void Package Repository
#v(1em)
*Void repository is a plain directory of static files served over HTTP(S):*
	+ \*.xbps package files, built by ./xbps-src pkg NAME into hostdir/binpkgs/.
	+ \*.xbps.sig2 signature files, one per package.
	+ x86_64-repodata, the signed index xbps downloads first.

xbps appends /x86_64-repodata to whatever URL is configured, so any host that serves these files from one directory URL works.

=== Creating the Void Package Repository isng the GitHub Platform

The GitHub Repository was chosen since I have had experience with the repsotiory in the past.

#table(
   columns: 3, 
   [*Option*], [*Pros*], [*Cons*], 
   [Raw URLs from a git repo], [Simplest], [Cached for minutes, rate limits, history bloat],
   [GitHub Pages (gh-pages branch)], [Stable CDN URL; force-push one commit to avoid bloat ], [1 GB limit; files over 100 MB rejected],
   [Github Releases], [No git bloat; 2 GB per asset; scales to a big repo], [Must replace assets (including repodata) on every update],
)

*Git Bloat*

Binaries committed to git stay in history forever, and each rebuild adds another full copy, since .xbps files are already compressed and 
cannot be diffed. For a big repo that means bloat and GitHub's soft ~1 GB repo guideline. 

Release assets avoid this: they are not part of git history, and a release tag acts as the "directory"



